# SYNAPSE Arsenal — the BlackArch category browser (GUI + TUI over one backend).
# It is in build.sh's PACKAGES because syn-install names it in SEL_APPS and an
# app the installer asks for that the local repo does not carry fails the
# install at pacman; that alone puts it in the ISO's [synapseos] repo, NOT in
# the live session. This line is what puts it in the live session, which is
# where every other SynapseOS app already is.
#
# It carries nothing heavy — depends are pacman/bash/curl, all present — and it
# does NOT enable [blackarch] by being here. The repo is enabled by the
# installer, or later with `sudo syn arsenal --enable-repo`; until then a live
# user gets Arsenal's own "the repo is not enabled" path rather than a browser
# with no packages behind it.
syn-arsenal
# synpkg — the SynapseOS package manager (repos + AUR + Flatpak + BlackArch +
# SynapseOS's own components, one C binary with a quickshell GUI and a terminal
# browser). Its hard depends are glibc/pacman/curl, all already present; the
# GUI, Flatpak and AUR paths are optdepends, so a headless install pays nothing
# for them and still gets the CLI.
#
# It is now the ONLY package manager front-end on the ISO. shelly-bin rode
# alongside it for one release while the replacement was phased; it is gone,
# and synpkg owns the start menu's Software Manager and Update System rows.
synpkg
# "About OS" — areofyl/fetch, which spins the SynapseOS mark as a lit 3D relief
# with the system info beside it. Reached from the control panel's
# System ▸ About OS row and from `syn about`; both run it in a terminal.
#
# It reads its logo ONLY from ~/.config/fetch/logo.txt (no system path), so the
# mark is COPIED there per user by customize_airootfs.sh here and by
# syn-install on an installed system — from the same
# /usr/share/synapseos/logo.txt that fastfetch renders flat. Without that file
# fetch falls through ID_LIKE to fastfetch's Arch logo, which is a perfectly
# good logo and the wrong distro's.
fetch
# foot — the rescue terminal, and the reason it is here is that it is a SECOND
# IMPLEMENTATION. 793 KiB of somebody else's code, so a bug that stops syntty
# opening still leaves a way to get a prompt on a live image and fix it.
foot
# syntty — our own terminal, and the default.
#
# It is 359 KB and links no GL at all: no EGL, no libLLVM, no gallium. A GL
# context is the one thing a live image cannot count on — an unfamiliar GPU, a
# driver that did not load, a VM with no acceleration — and a rescue disk that
# cannot open a prompt cannot rescue anything.
#
# ⛔ kitty IS NOT ON THIS LIST ANY MORE. It was, from the days it was the
# default, and the comment here still said so long after synui 359 made syntty
# the default and a hard dependency: 65 MiB of terminal that nothing opens,
# nothing names, and no fresh synuirc mentions — on the one image where the
# thing it needs, working OpenGL, is least likely to be there. It is an
# optdepend now, and `synpkg install kitty` puts it back on an installed
# system; every terminal chain in the suite still names it, so a box that
# predates this keeps working exactly as it did.
syntty
# Application launcher on Super+=. rofi 2.0.0 merged lbonn's Wayland port
# into mainline, so this is the plain `rofi` package (it Provides/Replaces
# rofi-wayland) talking layer-shell to synui — no XWayland, no AUR, no fork.
#
# It does NOT replace the cmdbar, which is native and carries the synsh intents
# and output capture; that has Super+Space (see seed_default_binds in
# synui/src/config.c). rofi is the plain "launch an app" key.
#
# Two Wayland-backend limitations upstream documents in the 2.0.0 notes: it
# cannot detect clicks outside its own surface, so clicking away does not
# dismiss it (Escape does), and it flickers on startup because scale is
# detected late.
rofi
firefox
# THE file manager, since 2026-08-10. Tabs, split view, pinned places shared
# with Dolphin's own bookmarks, trash, search and undo, in C plus quickshell,
# depending on nothing but glibc and shared-mime-info. It ships the
# distribution's /usr/share/applications/mimeapps.list, which is what actually
# makes a folder open in it.
synfiles
# The disk utility: what drives are in the machine, what is on them, their
# health, and mounting, safe removal and formatting. Reads the storage tree
# from /sys/class/block, so it works in a rescue boot with nothing running;
# everything that changes something is delegated to udisks2, smartmontools or
# polkit. Formatting anything on the disk holding / is refused outright.
syn-cal
syn-disks
syn-edit
syn-confine
# The password-locked folder. ⚠ IT WAS NEVER ON THIS LIST, though it has been in
# archiso/build.sh's PACKAGES since it shipped — so the ISO's local repo BUILT it
# and the live image never INSTALLED it. The two lists are independent and both
# are required; syn-install/tests/config_test.sh had been red about the other two
# surfaces this component was missing from.
#
# ⚠ It pulls gocryptfs, which is a hard dependency, onto the ISO with it. That is
# correct: a vault application installed without the thing that encrypts can
# refuse every command it has.
syn-vault
# Disk cleanup and secure delete. On the live image because "this disk is full"
# is a thing people boot a USB stick to deal with — the machine they are trying
# to clean being the one that will not start.
syn-clean
# The game assistant: the MangoHud overlay driven by a compositor keybind from
# inside a running game, plus controller detection, rumble, deadzone
# calibration and SDL mapping overrides. On the live ISO because "does my
# controller work on this machine" is a question worth answering BEFORE
# installing, and it is the one thing here that cannot be checked from a
# spec sheet.
syn-arcade
# The terminal music player big screen mode DRIVES. Every other player on its
# list is a tile that opens a window somebody then has to get out of with a
# gamepad; this one runs headless and takes transport over a socket, which is
# what makes Music on a television a tile that plays and a Start-menu row that
# controls it. Without it installed, that whole half of big screen mode is
# listed and absent — which is how it shipped, working on one machine.
cliamp
# The darkroom and edit suite: RAW develop, masks, a non-destructive sidecar,
# and a video timeline graded through the same colour engine. On the live ISO
# because "will this machine read my camera's raw files" is a question worth
# answering before installing — and because a live session someone is testing
# hardware from is exactly where they will want to look at a photograph.
synstudio
# GeForce NOW, in whichever Chromium-family browser is installed. Ships with no
# browser dependency of its own — it names what is missing rather than dragging
# a second browser onto the ISO beside Firefox.
#
# ⚠ THE OLD REASON FOR THAT LAST CLAUSE EXPIRED on 2026-08-19: the service no
# longer "refuses Firefox anyway". NVIDIA and Mozilla shipped GeForce NOW on
# Firefox 154 that day — on Windows. Linux Firefox browses the catalogue and
# starts no game, so the conclusion holds and the reason had to change. A live
# ISO session therefore cannot stream; syn-gfn says so and says what to
# install.
syn-gfn
# The settings app: displays, region, date and time, network, Bluetooth, power,
# kernels, default applications, and where configuration lives. It was in
# build-all.sh's component list, in syn-update's COMPONENTS and in the start
# menu, and NOT on this list — so it was built into the ISO's local repo on
# every release and never installed into the image. A fresh install had no
# Settings app at all until the first `syn-update apply` added one, which is
# the wrong way round: the pane that sets the keyboard layout is wanted before
# the pane that updates the system.
syn-settings
# dolphin is NOT here any more, as of 2026-08-13. It stopped being the default
# on 2026-08-10 and then had no job left: synfiles is THE file manager, and it
# reads $XDG_DATA_DIRS/kio/servicemenus itself, so synui's service menus (Mount
# Image, Extract, Crop, Set as Wallpaper, Run with Wine) show up without it.
# What it still cost is 45 packages and ~160MB installed — baloo, kio-extras,
# kfilemetadata, poppler, ripgrep-all and the rest of its own tree, measured as
# dolphin's dependency closure minus kdeconnect's, NOT the ~550MB the whole
# Qt6+KF6 stack is usually quoted at: kdeconnect below pays for that part and
# stays. `synpkg install dolphin` is one command for anyone who wants it.
#
# The two /etc/xdg files synui ships for KDE-outside-Plasma stay and still
# matter — kdeconnect below is a KF6 app: menus/applications.menu (or every
# file opens the "Open With" chooser) and kdeglobals (or "Open Terminal"
# silently does nothing, KIO's fallback being a konsole we don't ship).
# Phone pairing: notifications, file transfer, clipboard, remote control.
# It carries the KF6 tree on its own now that dolphin is off the image — the
# parts that would normally need theming work are
# already SynapseOS's: the phone's notifications go through knotifications to
# org.freedesktop.Notifications, which synui itself serves and draws, and the
# indicator is a StatusNotifierItem, which the quickshell bar already hosts.
#
# The daemon is what has to be running for a phone to reach the desktop, and
# the /etc/xdg/autostart file the package ships for it is DEAD HERE: nothing in
# a synui session implements the XDG autostart spec (synui's autostart is
# synuirc `autostart =` lines). /etc/synui/synuirc starts the indicator, which
# D-Bus-activates the daemon and supplies the tray icon in one go.
kdeconnect
gamescope
# The bar. Neither of these was on the ISO, so the live session had no bar at
# all — no clock, no tray, no network indicator — while an installed system had
# one; that was an oversight, not a decision. synui pulls quickshell in as a
# dependency now, but list it explicitly: what the ISO ships should be readable
# here rather than inferred from someone else's depends. wtype is how the bar's
# SYNAPSE button asks synui to open the start menu (there is no other IPC in).
#
# quickshell replaced waybar as the bar on 2026-07-24 (synui/systemd/synui-bar.sh
# records why). waybar is deliberately absent: nothing starts it, and the ISO is
# already ~7GB.
quickshell
wtype

# Screen recording (Super+Shift+R). synui pulls it in as a dependency; listed
# here so what the ISO ships is readable rather than inferred.
wf-recorder

# Printing. SynapseOS shipped a desktop and a browser and no way to print from
# either. cups brings its own web admin UI on localhost:631, so there is no
# printer-setup GUI to write or ship — the start menu just opens it.
#
# cups pulls avahi itself, which is most of what makes this work: a printer made
# since ~2015 speaks IPP Everywhere / AirPrint, so cups discovers it over mDNS
# and prints with no driver at all. nss-mdns is the glibc half of that — without
# it nothing resolves the printer's own .local name. ghostscript is what renders
# PDF for printers that don't do it themselves; cups-pdf adds a "Print to PDF"
# printer, which is the one every machine should have.
cups
cups-pdf
ghostscript
nss-mdns
# The driverless-IPP story above covers any printer since ~2015. The Xpress
# M2020W predates it: an SPL device cups cannot drive without Samsung's ULD
# rastertospl filter.
#
# samsung-m2020 is NOT here any more, and deliberately. Samsung's EULA licenses
# the ULD binaries "strictly for the personal use" and states that "No other
# use, copying or distribution of the SOFTWARE PRODUCT is permitted" — an ISO
# carrying them is distribution, whatever the build machine downloaded. It is
# now an on-demand install instead: `syn printer samsung` fetches the driver
# from Samsung onto the machine that will print with it, which is the thing the
# licence actually describes. Same reason the AUR ships a recipe, not the files.

# Bluetooth. bluez is the daemon (org.bluez); bluez-utils is bluetoothctl, which
# synui does not need — it talks BlueZ over sd-bus itself (synui/src/bt.c) — but
# which is the only way to debug a radio from a shell when the panel says no.
bluez
bluez-utils

# Wine so the live environment can run Windows .exe/.msi, and so the packages
# are cached on the ISO for the installer's pacstrap (syn-install adds them to
# the target). wine-mono covers .NET-based installers.
wine
wine-mono
virtualbox-guest-utils
dkms
xdg-desktop-portal
xdg-desktop-portal-wlr
xdg-desktop-portal-gtk
slurp
grim
rtkit

# Wallpaper Engine backgrounds (the Workshop rows in synui's Super+W picker,
# driven by the synui-wpengine script this package also ships). Built from
# linux-wallpaperengine-pkg/PKGBUILD against a pinned upstream commit; it is in
# no pacman repo, so pacstrap can only get it from our local-repo.
#
# synui does NOT depend on it — the picker simply shows no Workshop rows when
# it is absent — so this line is the only thing putting it on the ISO. It is
# listed rather than left off because the wallpapers come from Steam Workshop
# subscriptions, and a user who has just subscribed to one should not then have
# to build a CEF-carrying package by hand to see it.
linux-wallpaperengine
# SynapseOS' own Wallpaper Engine wallpapers (dendrite + Tux), installed to
# /usr/share/synapse/wallpapers/431960 — the SYSROOT synui-wpengine.sh:48
# searches after the user's Workshop tree. Same shape as the line above:
# nothing depends on it, so this line is the only thing putting it on the ISO.
synapse-wallpapers
btrfs-progs
xfsprogs
f2fs-tools
snapper
snap-pac
grub-btrfs
inotify-tools
limine
limine-snapper-sync
# limine-mkinitcpio-hook IS DELIBERATELY NOT LISTED HERE, and must never be
# added back. It writes limine boot entries for installed kernels, which a
# limine install needs — without it `pacman -S linux-lts` yields a complete,
# correct, unbootable kernel. syn-install pacman -S's it into the TARGET at
# install time (syn-install.sh), and it reaches that target through the local
# repo, which build.sh fills from its own PACKAGES array. Being in the repo and
# being in this file are separate things; only this file INSTALLS a package into
# the live ISO. synapse-llama-cuda is in the repo and not here for the same
# reason (build.sh).
#
# Listing it here BREAKS THE BUILD, and not in the place it is listed. The
# package ships /etc/pacman.d/hooks/90-mkinitcpio-install.hook, the same basename
# as mkinitcpio's /usr/share/libalpm/hooks/90-mkinitcpio-install.hook, and /etc
# wins. Identical triggers; the Exec differs:
#
#   Arch:   /usr/share/libalpm/scripts/mkinitcpio install
#   limine: /usr/share/libalpm/scripts/limine-mkinitcpio-install
#
# Arch's script does TWO jobs — it copies /usr/lib/modules/$kver/vmlinuz to the
# path the preset names (/boot/vmlinuz-linux) AND builds the initramfs. Replaced
# by limine's, which writes limine entries and finds no limine install in a
# chroot, NEITHER file is ever produced: the pacstrap dir's /boot stays empty
# with the kernel package installed and every surface reporting success.
#
# The build then dies four steps later in mkarchiso's _make_boot_on_iso9660, on
# `install -- "${pacstrap_dir}/boot/initramfs-"*".img"` — a glob that matches
# nothing — with nothing in the message pointing back here. That is what broke
# 0.2.8: the package was added to this file on 2026-08-11 (2c87e69), and the ISO
# had not been rebuilt since 0.2.6.
zram-generator

# nix — ONLY so `syn-install --config profile.nix` can evaluate a Nix install
# profile on the install medium itself. Nothing on the ISO uses Nix otherwise,
# and the installed system gets it only if WANT_NIX was taken.
#
# Without this line the .nix path still works, it just has to be rendered
# somewhere else first (`syn nix profile p.nix > install.conf`) — so if the
# ~30 MB is not worth it, deleting this line costs only that convenience and
# the installer already says so when nix is missing.
nix

# Security auditing. Both are small (arch-audit ~4.8 MB, lynis ~1.7 MB) and
# both are on the live ISO deliberately: auditing an install you have not
# booted yet is the case where you least want to be fetching tools.
#
# arch-audit closes Lynis PKGS-7398 (package vulnerability check) and is the
# first check syn-secure's hardening pane will call. lynis reads the
# skip-test profile that synapse_kmod ships at /etc/lynis/custom.prf, which is
# what keeps the hardening index a signal rather than a constant.
arch-audit
lynis

# ⛔ THE MENU'S ICONS. synui's .desktop entries name standard freedesktop icons;
# a standard name still needs a package to provide the file, and neither theme
# was on this list. The live ISO showed the same half-blank apps menu a clean
# install does — invisible on the build machine, where other software had
# dragged both in. 22 of the names come from breeze-icons and 15 from
# adwaita-icon-theme-legacy, so both are needed. synui depends on them too;
# they are here so the LIVE session has them before anything is installed.
breeze-icons
adwaita-icon-theme-legacy
